            "type": "reasoning",
            "content": [
              {
                "text": "I'll design a Sierpinski-style fractal template myself using a simple 2x2 pattern, then expand it recursively into 4x4 and 8x8 grids by substituting filled cells with copies of the base template.\n\nFor the 4x4 case I'm working out the pixel grid by tiling the 2x2 pattern into each filled block, verifying the result forms 1000/1100/1010/1111, then mapping filled cells to 5 and adding a 2 as a highlight marker at one corner.\n\nI'm also checking that alternative block-size decompositions (like 4x4 blocks of 1x1) wouldn't be chosen over the smaller 2x2 template since they'd cover a larger area unnecessarily, then planning a second demo using an 8x8 grid built from a different 2x2 template with its own highlighted cells.\n\nNow I'm expanding a 2x3 template at level 2 into a 4x6 grid, tracing how each block copies the template's 1,0,1 / 1,1,1 pattern into the appropriate rows and recalculating the width since three template-width blocks give 9 columns, not 6 as I first assumed.\n\nThen I'm working through Demo3, an 8x8 grid built from a 2x2 template at level 3, expanding it first into a 4x4 intermediate grid where blocks (0,0), (0,1), and (1,1) get filled with the template pattern.\n\nThen I extend that same block pattern into the full 8x8: the top-left and top-right quadrants both get the 4x4 pattern, while the bottom-left is all zeros and the bottom-right gets the 4x4 pattern again. Checking the highlight coordinates, (0,0) maps to (0,0) and (7,7) maps to (1,1) within the base template.\n\nFor a fourth demo, I try a simpler diagonal identity template at 2x2, then expand it to 4x4 by placing identity blocks along the diagonal, and again to 8x8 the same way, giving a clean staircase pattern. I highlight (4,4) which maps back to (0,0) in the base template, giving output [[2,0],[0,5]] \u2014 that's four solid demo variations now, so I consider adding a fifth with a non-square 3x2 template just to broaden coverage.\n\nI build the 3x2 template with blocks at (0,0), (0,1), (1,1), (2,0) filled and expand each into 3x2 sub-blocks, checking each 3-row band fills correctly with the right blocks marked and others zeroed. The highlighted cell at (8,0) maps back to template position (2,0), which is indeed a 1, giving output [[5,5],[0,5],[2,0]] \u2014 that rounds out five demos total to present.\n\n",
                "type": "reasoning_text"
              }
            ],
